<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lippe (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lippe is a river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 220 km long, rising near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> and flowing west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wesel">Wesel</a>. In antiquity it was a main route of Roman advances into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> bases such as Oberaden and Haltern along its banks. It gave its name to the County of Lippe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lippe-river.jpg" alt="Lippe (river)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Hunscher, Dortmund · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lippe_in_Luenen_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Lippe is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and a right tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. It is 220.3 km long, with an elevation difference of 125 metres and a catchment area of 4.890 km². The source lies at the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-lippspringe">Bad Lippspringe</a>, close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a>. The river runs westward through Paderborn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lippstadt">Lippstadt</a>, then along the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr area</a>, parallel to the Emscher and the Ruhr, and finally enters the Rhine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wesel">Wesel</a>. Its towns include Wesel, Dorsten, Marl, Lünen and Haltern, and its cities Paderborn, Lippstadt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamm">Hamm</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Roman times the river, called Lupia, was a gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a>. It carried supplies, and many Roman camps stood along its banks. Over the last 200 years many have been identified, above all the camp at Haltern, likely the headquarters of the imperial prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. The camps lay about a day's walk apart for the troops, almost 30 km. Those identified are Olfen, Xanten, Haltern, Oberaden, Holsterhausen, Anreppen and Beckinghausen. The Battle of the Lupia River was fought in 11 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero-claudius-drusus">Nero Claudius Drusus</a> and the Sicambri.</p><p>The Lippe was partly navigable from 1820, but not enough for the transport of industrial goods. The landscape changed with the Cologne-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> trunk line of the 1840s, which linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> cities with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> harbours and the Lippe town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamm">Hamm</a>, and supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>. Hard coal mining, begun in the middle of the 19th century in the Emscher area, spread north into the Lippe catchment, and from the 1860s mining subsidence and polluted drainage became problems. Hamm, Lünen, Haltern and Dorsten, small settlements, expanded with new mines from about 1900.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Industrial pollution led to the founding of water boards, the Sesekegenossenschaft in 1913 and the Lippeverband in 1926. The Datteln-Hamm Canal (1914) and the Wesel-Datteln Canal (1930) run parallel to the river, which is not navigable for mass transport, and exchange water with it. Upstream of Hamm the catchment is more rural, and downstream settlement and industry mark it. Water pollution played little part in public debate until the 1970s; environmental standards, above all from the EU, have since led to efforts to improve the river's condition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lippe (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bructeri lived between the upper Ems and the <b>Lippe</b>, in what is now Westphalia.</blockquote><a href="/state/bructeri">Bructeri</a></li><li><blockquote>Their county lay between the Ruhr and the <b>Lippe</b>, and they long fought the archbishops of Cologne for dominance in Westphalia.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-mark">County of Mark</a></li><li><blockquote>Their lands later stretched from the <b>Lippe</b> to the Ems and beyond to the Dutch border.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-munster">Prince-Bishopric of Münster</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sugambri lived on the east bank of the Rhine between the Sieg and the <b>Lippe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sugambri">Sugambri</a></li></ul></section>
